<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Ali Wallajah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah was Nawab of the Carnatic from 1749 to 1795. Backed by the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> against his French-supported rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a>, he won the throne through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnatic-wars">Carnatic Wars</a>, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> in 1751. He moved his court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, but heavy debts to the Company and its servants left him ever more dependent on the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-wallajah.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Smart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Muhammad_%27Ali_Khan,_Nawab_of_Arcot_and_Prince_of_the_Carnatic_(1718-1795)_(by_John_Smart).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wallajah was born on 7 July 1717, the son of Anwaruddin Muhammed Khan, Nawab of the Carnatic, whose family was of Qannauji Sheikh origin. Muhammad Ali Khan later claimed Arab and Sayyid descent, but a modern study of the Walajah dynasty argues that the family was of indigenous origin and that he deliberately built a genealogy linking it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a>. His mother, Fakhr un-nisa Begum Sahiba, was the niece of Sayyid Ali Khan Safavi ul-Mosawi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When his father was killed at the Battle of Ambur in 1749, Wallajah fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Trichinopoly</a>, and his claim was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix">Joseph François Dupleix</a> and the French. British reinforcements under Stringer Lawrence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> came to his aid in April 1752, forcing the besiegers of Trichinopoly to withdraw to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, and Chanda Sahib surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a>. Emperor Shah Alam II gave Wallajah further titles in 1760, and the Treaty of Paris recognised him as an independent ruler in 1763.</p><p>He harboured ambitions of his own in a south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> were constant rivals, and his failure to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> to Hyder Ali as promised lay at the root of many later conflicts between Mysore and the British. Much of the war that followed Hyder Ali's invasion of the Carnatic on 23 July 1780 was fought on the Nawab's lands. For the defence of his territory he paid the British 400,000 pagodas a year, about £160,000, and 10 of the 21 battalions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> army garrisoned his forts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1764 Wallajah planned a palace inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St. George</a>, but there was no room, and Chepauk Palace was built for him instead by Paul Benfield, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> engineer turned contractor, and completed in 1768. One of the first buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the Indo-Saracenic style, it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> as the capital until 1855. The Nawab borrowed heavily, and because many Company officials were his creditors his affairs became a factor in Westminster politics. He died of gangrene at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> on 13 October 1795 and was succeeded by his son Umdat ul-Umara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rival claims between Chanda Sahib, backed by the French, and <b>Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah</b>, backed by the British, led to the Carnatic Wars and the siege of Arcot in 1751, a turning point in British fortunes.</blockquote><a href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Carnatic Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1767" aria-label="Delhi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6568 1115 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7018 1385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.67,77.22&amp;year=1767">What was here in 1767? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
